<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anathapindika</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sudatta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anathapindika, whose personal name was Sudatta, was a wealthy merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a> in Kosala and the Buddha's foremost lay supporter. According to Buddhist tradition he bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jetavana">Jetavana</a> grove from Prince Jeta by covering its ground with gold coins and built a monastery there for the Buddha and his monks. His name means 'giver of alms to the unprotected'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anathapindika.jpg" alt="Anathapindika" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beglar, Joseph David, 1875 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jetvan_bharhut.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
